+## Testbenches around the cell
+
+Seen in Christoph Maier's hand-drawn level-shifter testbenches (2026-10-04). The wire rules above
+apply unchanged.
+
+- Left to right: sources, device under test (DUT), loads. The DUT sits close to the sources, a few
+  hundred units away.
+- Sources are vertical, + on top, minus down to the ground bus. From left to right: the highest
+  supply, the lower supplies, then the input signal.
+- Supply and input nets are wires run above the DUT in nested L-shaped paths: the leftmost source
+  (highest supply) gets the topmost wire, the next source the next one down, the input the lowest,
+  so they do not cross on the way to the DUT. At the DUT the lower supply goes to the left supply
+  pin of the symbol, so the nesting carries into the pins. Accept a crossing only where it is
+  unavoidable (a second DUT further right that needs the lower supply).
+- One ground bus along the bottom, with a single `gnd` symbol at its left end. Source minus pins,
+  DUT ground pins and load capacitors are wired to it; no `gnd` symbol per element.
+- A load hangs below the output wire: a wire from the DUT output down to the top of the capacitor,
+  the bottom of the capacitor on the ground bus. The node between two DUTs is a straight wire with
+  its load hanging from a junction.
+- Name each net with a `lab_pin` sitting on its wire, at the source and at the DUT end (`vddh`,
+  `vddl`, `in`, `out`), not on a stub.
+- Title at the top left, the analysis code block (`code_shown`) under the circuit at the left.
+
